prompt:
You are a compliance referee. Explain this cross-department gap to a launch manager in 2-3 plain sentences and give 2-3 concrete fix options. Use ONLY these facts; do not invent any.
Seam type: data_residency_breach
Title: 1 supplier(s) process customer data outside UK/EEA, against the customer terms
Departments: Finance, Legal, Product
Facts: {"allowed_regions": ["UK", "EEA"], "breaches": ["V002 StreamLedger Inc: customer payment data processed in US-East (United States); C-2026-031 DPA signed=True"], "promise": "Customer payment data is processed and stored only within the UK or EEA, including by sub-processors."}
Return JSON: {"explanation": str, "fix_options": [str]}

response:
```json
{
  "explanation": "Our customer terms promise that customer payment data is processed and stored only within the UK or EEA, including by sub-processors. However, supplier V002 StreamLedger Inc processes customer payment data in US-East (United States), which breaks that promise even though its DPA (C-2026-031) is signed. Finance, Legal and Product each own part of this, so the gap sits between teams and needs a joint decision before launch.",
  "fix_options": [
    "Product and Finance ask StreamLedger Inc to move processing of our customer payment data to a UK or EEA region. Legal then confirms the change under DPA C-2026-031.",
    "Finance and Product replace StreamLedger Inc with a supplier that processes customer payment data only in the UK or EEA.",
    "Legal revises the customer terms to disclose and permit processing in the United States by sub-processors. This only works if the business accepts changing the promise to customers."
  ]
}
```
